==== Wave 2: Mechanics

The Challenger 650 is the aircraft that teaches you the systems every professional turbine pilot works with for the rest of their career: turbofan engines that largely manage themselves, a cabin that flies at a different altitude than you do, an FMS that does most of the navigation, and the instrument discipline that makes all of it safe in the weather. None of it is hard on its own — the skill is in how the pieces fit together, and that is what this chapter is for.

===== Turbofan Power

The engines under the Challenger's wings are a different species from the SR22's piston. A turbofan breathes: air enters the big fan at the front, and most of it bypasses the core to produce thrust directly, while the rest feeds the core compressor, the combustor, and the turbines that drive both the fan and the core. That is why the two speeds on the engine display matter. N1 is the fan speed, and in practice it is your *thrust gauge*: in almost every regime you fly, "power" means an N1 target, not a lever position. N2 is the core speed — the "engine is alive and healthy" number: it spins up with the starter, settles into a stable idle, and tracks its own limits as you fly.

What makes a turbofan feel different to fly is how it responds to your hand. A piston engine answers instantly; a turbofan *spools*. Push the levers forward and the thrust builds over a couple of seconds; pull them back and it decays slowly. Every power change in the Challenger has to be anticipated rather than reacted to — on the approach you set thrust for where the aircraft will be five seconds from now, not where it is now. The auto-throttle (where your installation has it) smooths much of this for you by holding an N1 target, but the habit of thinking ahead of the spool is yours to build, because it is the same habit you will need in the Q400 and the 737.

The third number on the engine page is EGT, exhaust gas temperature, and it is the engine's limit in human terms. EGT climbs with power, with heat, and with slow speed — a hot day, a heavy aircraft, and a shallow climb all push it up — and the rule is simple: never hold it in the red. Watch EGT the way you watched the stall warning in Wave 0: not the primary instrument, but the one that tells you when you are asking too much of the machine.

===== Pressurization & Cabin

The cabin flies at a different altitude than you do, and that is a system you manage. Bleed air tapped from the engines pressurizes the fuselage; the pressurization controller lets you set the *cabin altitude* you want, and it then climbs and descends the cabin at a gentle, comfortable rate while the aircraft does its own, faster thing. The client in the back seat feels the cabin, not the aircraft — so the rule of the charter pilot is that cabin rate limits your climb and descent, not the flight path. Set the cabin altitude target for the cruise altitude before you climb, and the controller does the rest.

The same system makes the descent a planning exercise. A turbine aircraft can come down very fast; the cabin cannot. A good arrival starts with a top-of-descent calculated so the cabin pressure and the flight path arrive at the airport together — begin down too late and you either rush the cabin down or float high and fast at the runway. It is the first hint of the energy management that becomes the whole game in later waves: plan the descent, then fly the plan.

===== FMS & Navigation

The Challenger's flight deck — a Pro Line 21-style suite of large displays and a full flight management system — is the cockpit of the rest of your career. The FMS is a route computer: you load a plan into it (the same plan you built in LittleNavMap), add the departure and arrival procedures, and it flies the lateral and vertical path, turn by turn and step by step, while you manage the aircraft and the decisions around it. The important mental model is that the FMS is *your* tool, not a substitute for you: it executes exactly what you entered, so entering it carefully and cross-checking it are the skills that matter.

The approaches you fly with it come in two families. RNAV/GPS approaches let the box guide you along a defined path with vertical guidance — you watch the box do the navigating while you manage speed and configuration. ILS approaches give you the classic needles: a localizer for lateral alignment and a glideslope for the vertical path, captured and followed by hand or by the flight director. Fly both until the difference is instinct — a pilot who can only follow one is a pilot who is one weather system away from being stuck.

===== IFR Discipline

Instrument flying is a set of habits, and Wave 2 is where they become permanent. The first is the scan: a constant, deliberate sweep of attitude, airspeed, altitude, heading, and vertical speed, cross-checked against the approach plate's numbers. The second is the culture of minimums — every approach has a decision altitude and a visibility minimum, and below them the go-around is not a choice, it is the procedure; the professional's pride is in flying the procedure, not in "making" the approach. The third is radio discipline: read back clearances, report positions, and let the sim's ATC keep you honest — the quiet confidence of someone who files and flies the system is an instrument in itself.

And the fourth habit is the weather itself. Anti-ice and de-icing equipment (engine heat, wing heat) buys you the *escape* from icing, never the right to push through it; ice is a leave condition, and the plan should include the way out before you fly into the cloud.

===== Workload Management

Single-pilot IFR in a business jet is a course in triage, and the triage order never changes: *aviate, navigate, communicate*. When the workload spikes — a busy arrival, a weather decision, a system caution — the airplane and the path come first, and the radio waits. Flows and checklists support this: you do the flow from memory (the same sequence every time, which is what makes it memory), then confirm against the list. Delegate to the systems that earn it — the autopilot holds altitude, the FMS flies the path — and keep the decisions that are yours: weather, fuel, minimums, and the go-around. The aircraft flies the flight; you fly the operation.
